# A field outside its range (month 00 or 13, day 32, hour 24, minute 60, second above 60, too many digits) and a date that does not exist
# (Feb 29 of a common year, Feb 30, Apr 31): ours and GNU exit with an error; macOS in POSIXLY_CORRECT mode takes mktime's answer (Feb 30 is Mar 2).
-d 1900-02-29T00:00:00Z f
-d 2007-02-29T10:15:30 f
-d 2007-02-30T10:15:30 f
-d 2007-04-31T10:15:30 f
-d 2100-02-29T00:00:00Z f
-t -1010000 f
-t +1010000 f
-t 00000000 f
-t 00000000.00 f
-t 00010000 f
-t 01002400 f
-t 01010000.61 f
-t 01010000.6x f
-t 01010000.99 f
-t 0101000000 f
-t 0101000a f
-t 01010060 f
-t 01010060.00 f
-t 01012400 f
-t 01320000 f
-t 02290000.00 f
-t 02300000.00 f
-t 02310000 f
-t 04310000 f
-t 06310000 f
-t 09310000 f
-t 11310000 f
-t 1231232359 f
-t 13010000 f
-t 13320000 f
-t 190002290000 f
-t 200102290000 f
-t 2007-11-12 f
-t 210002290000 f
-t 99999999 f
-t 99999999.99 f
# The format has two digits in every field and a single space (or T) between date and time; macOS (and GNU) take a tab, two blanks, one digit.
-d '2007-11-12	10:15:30' f
-d '2007-11-12  10:15:30' f
-d 2007-11-12T10:15:3 f
-d 2007-11-1T10:15:30 f
# A time the file system cannot hold: "touch shall exit immediately with an error status"; macOS says success and stores 1969-12-31T23:59:59 (before 1970) or a clamped time.
-d 0000-01-01T00:00:00Z f
-d 0001-01-01T00:00:00Z f
-d 0100-03-01T00:00:00Z f
-d 1000-01-01T00:00:00Z f
-d 1582-10-04T00:00:00Z f
-d 1582-10-15T00:00:00Z f
-d 2400-02-29T00:00:00Z f
-d 2400-02-29T00:00:00Z f
-t 229912312359 f
-t 229912312359 f
# Representable (the earliest APFS time) and written correctly by ours and GNU; macOS stores 1969-12-31T23:59:59.
-d 1677-09-21T00:12:43Z f
